# NOTE: `.github/` was ignored here from 2026-07-20 (77c307e, a Supabase-auth
# commit that had nothing to do with CI — collateral, not a decision) until
# 2026-08-14. android-build.yml survived only because it was already tracked;
# any NEW file under .github/ — a second workflow, CODEOWNERS, dependabot config
# — would have been dropped silently, with `git status` showing a clean tree.
# Do not re-add: CI configuration is exactly the kind of thing that must be
# versioned, and nothing secret belongs under .github/ in the first place.

# Firebase client config (Phase 2 / FCM push wake-up). Not a hard secret — it
# ships inside the APK — but it names the Firebase project and its API keys,
# which is not something to publish in a repo. Each developer/CI drops their
# own copy into app/. See app/build.gradle.kts:68 and AGENTS.md → "Push
# wake-up". The Voximplant control-panel upload uses a DIFFERENT file (a
# service-account JSON from the Firebase console); that one is a real secret
# and must never be in this repo at all.
app/google-services.json
google-services.json

# Release signing keystore. A build that finds no KEYSTORE_PATH silently falls
# back to debug.keystore (verified empirically 14.8), so a committed upload key
# would be both a leak and a source of confusion about which key signed what.
# debug.keystore is already ignored by name above; these cover the release one
# under any name.
*.jks
*.keystore

# Claude Code session data — EXCEPT project agents/skills/settings, which are
# team-shared and belong in the repo. Same shape as beta's .gitignore
# deliberately: two repos that treat .claude/ oppositely is a trap of its own.
#
# The concrete hazard this closes is .claude/worktrees/ — an agent worktree is
# a full second copy of the app (1.6M when this was written), registered with
# git and showing up untracked on main, one `git add -A` from being committed
# by whoever runs one next.
.claude/*
!.claude/agents/
!.claude/skills/
!.claude/settings.json

# Voximplant session logs. `npm run voximplant -- log --session <id>` is run
# from whichever repo happens to be the working directory, and it drops
# `session-<id>.log` at that root. Two of them landed here untracked and were
# one `git add -A` away from being committed.
#
# They carry guest PII: the called number in full, and the scenario's own log
# lines for what was said on the call. beta's .gitignore has covered this since
# 15.8; this repo's did not, which is the whole reason they showed up here.
/session-*.log
